   308	## Gate results
   309	
   310	### Gate A — PASS
   311	
   312	- A1: the exact translated body is bound in the closure and executes under
   313	  fixed semantics. The AST identity check passes; the changed-body claim fails.
   314	- A2: there are no operational bridges. The loop claim explicitly tracks every
   315	  state cell it reads or changes.
   316	- A3: module and builtin bindings, evaluation, call frame, continuation, and
   317	  return behavior are pinned. The guarded dispatch is term-local after fixed
   318	  call routing and has an independently checked fixed equation.
   319	- A4: every proof-local total function has constructor-exhaustive or unguarded
   320	  coverage. Guards are disjoint or overlapping equations agree. Structural
   321	  recursions descend.
   322	- A5: realizable witnesses include empty input and `["1"]`; false result, body,
   323	  and count interpretations are all rejected.
   324	
   325	### Gate B — PASS
   326	
   327	- B1: the empty/nonempty entry partition and recursive circularity cover every
   328	  finite list and every finite digit-string length. There is no chosen bound.
   329	- B2: the contract uses only digit input and ASCII output, all represented by
   330	  the supplied code-sequence model. K integers and CPython integers are
   331	  unbounded for this task.
   332	- B3: the execution summary is connected directly to supplied `str.count`,
   333	  integer-to-string conversion, string concatenation, and list append. On a
   334	  digit-only string, the five counted characters are exactly the odd digits.
   335	- B4: the implementation matches the prompt's examples, including repeated
   336	  substitution and a multi-digit count.
   337	
   338	### Gate C — PASS
   339	
   340	- All proof-local extensions and trust assumptions are inventoried.
   341	- Every reported command, output, mutation, and differential result has an
   342	  existing artifact and is replayed by `prove.sh`.
   343	- Formal proof, fixed-semantics assumptions, finite evidence, and excluded
   344	  behavior are separated here.
   345	
   346	## Trust boundary
   347	
   348	- The supplied read-only `reference-semantics/` definition is trusted as the
   349	  Python subset model. In particular, `cntSub`, `strToCodes`, `Int2String`,
   350	  `seqConcat`, `valSeqConcat`, call routing, scoping, allocation, and append are
   351	  outside this theorem's rederivation. They affect values, control, and state;
   352	  all target claims depend on them.
   353	- The K compiler, Haskell backend, LLVM backend, SMT reasoning, and host
   354	  runtime are trusted.
   355	- `Int2String` is a fixed K hook used by the supplied `str(int)` rule. It
   356	  affects the returned decimal text. Concrete and differential evidence checks
   357	  relevant values, including a count greater than 9, but finite tests are not a
   358	  universal proof of the hook.
   359	- The theorem is conditional on normal termination, as required by the Kit's
   360	  partial-correctness model.
   361	
   362	No additional trusted primitive or result-bearing oracle was introduced in
   363	`verification.k`.
   364	
   365	## Empirically supported facts
   366	
   367	`validation.py` uses an independent CPython oracle:
